\f1\i\fs22 Gerry Hardman\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>G. Hardman\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jihad Man\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f2\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Ajent Jones scribbled on the cocktail napkin and slammed the pencil down so hard it was a wonder neither it nor the bar top broke. He drained the glass half-empty, slammed it down, and demanded of the barkeep, \'93Another.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender hesitated. \'93Sir, with respect, perhaps you\'92ve consumed a sufficiency?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s just tonic.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender looked nervously at the bin under the counter, brimming with napkins Agent Jones had, the past ninety minutes or so, scribbled on with an officious and altogether intimidating violence. If anything, he was growing angrier. \'93Well, sure,\'94 the bartender conceded, \'93but it\'92s zero sugar. That stuff\'92ll rot your kidneys.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I don\'92t consume alcohol,\'94 Agent Jones said, as if this settled all health concerns.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Good thing too
\f2\i0 , the bartender didn\'92t say. 
\f1\i Who knows what this blockhead would be like drunk.
\f2\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones glared. The bartender wilted. \'93No ice, right?\'94 he said sunnily as he grabbed a glass and pushed the soda gun into it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ice is an adulterous garnish,\'94 Agent Jones sermonised as he smoothed his black tie.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, yes. I recall you saying that before.\'94 The bartender arranged a fresh napkin before Agent Jones, and laid the tall glass of tonic upon it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones gestured with four fingers. More.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender frisbeed a half-inch wad of folded napkins across the bar. It stopped spinning near Agent Jones\'92 left bank of knuckles. Agent Jones took a napkin from the top and placed it ready for scribbling.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It all connects,\'94 he muttered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He wrote 
\f1\i GYGES
\f2\i0  in block capitals. Stared at it. In the dim greasy light of the bar, his graphite notes twinkled silver, making them hard to read. But Agent Jones knew what the words read, which is why he stopped writing. If he\'92d followed 
\f1\i GYGES
\f2\i0  with 
\f1\i DISC
\f2\i0 , he\'92d have to eat the napkin, or ram it down the bartender\'92s gullet like he was a foie gras duck. One corner of Agent Jones\'92 mouth almost curled into a smile at the prospect, but he arrested it on the parade ground of his face before any of the other features destabilised.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With jagged lightning strikes of the pencil, he crossed out the word, then wrote 
\f1\i ELOISE
\f2\i0 .\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tax, I\'92m losing it!
\f2\i0  he swore under his breath, and broke the pencil in three places. With a complete absence of grace, he clutched the napkin and scrunched it into a shape resembling a dumpling, and pushed it two knuckles into his tonic. The liquid surged over the top until he retrieved the napkin, which he swallowed in one awkward, indelicate gulp.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sir, would you like to see the menu?\'94 the bartender asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Quack, quack,\'94 Agent Jones said through the pulp in his mouth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender couldn\'92t parse the ill-pronounced words and, fearing Agent Jones was choking, quickly topped his glass up. The last thing he needed was a customer choking to death on his shift. Especially if he\'92d failed to sell the customer any bar snacks when he was clearly hungry and/or demented. Either way, management would penalise him for the lost sale.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It all connects.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones grabbed another napkin and wrote 
\f1\i TRAVIS TEH
\f2\i0 . Drew a line. 
\f1\i BLANK
\f2\i0 . Another line. 
\f1\i DEAD
\f2\i0 . He crossed out 
\f1\i BLANK
\f2\i0  so hard the pencil tore through the napkin.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>From the beginning, intelligence pointed to Shakti Enterprises. The Gyges Disc was always Shakti\'92s play\'97had to be. PAPER had set up a Blank Contract to retrieve it from Teh. The blank\'97Agent Jones couldn\'92t remember his name, which was exactly the point of Blank Contracts\'97had blown Teh away. Jones had seen the evidence. But the blank never showed for the hand-off. Dead the next morning. Street crime, supposedly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Supposedly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>No Disc.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jones wrote 
\f1\i DISC
\f2\i0  on a fresh napkin, stared at it, then scribbled it out so violently the pencil point snapped. He grabbed another pencil from the pile the bartender had provided earlier, now dwindling.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sir?\'94 the bartender ventured. \'93Perhaps some peanuts?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jones ignored him.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>SARAH JACOBSON
\f2\i0 , he wrote. 
\f1\i ANTHONY CHANTARA
\f2\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It grew more complicated from there. Jacobson was the one mentioned in that cabinet meeting\'97the Holy Momma whipping up religious fervour among the Elvi. And they\'92d broken Chantara out of Truth Central. Using tanks. Shakti tanks. Then Eloise Shakti herself\'97herself!\'97had flicked a switch and killed every unfriendly weapon in range. TRAMPS and PAPER both.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones was sure Eloise Shakti had acquired the Gyges Disc during the siege, if she didn\'92t possess it already. He scrawled \f1\i ELOISE = DISC????\f2\i0  across the napkin, the question marks breeding like her subsidiaries. Every arms customer she\'92d betrayed would be sharpening something with her name on it. And if she were forging new weapons from Gyges technology\'97weapons only she could sell\'97then the arms race wasn\'92t ending. It was metastasising.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender, edging closer with a damp cloth and the tentative body language of a man defusing a bomb in a customer service role, caught a glimpse of the napkin and retreated so fast his elbow clipped the soda gun. He had seventeen minutes until shift change and was beginning to doubt he would survive them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jones flipped the napkin and ground \f1\i MUSIC\f2\i0  into the back with a pencil stub he\'92d been gnawing. A splinter of yellow paint lodged between his front teeth. He didn\'92t notice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A new fear gripped him. If war broke out, the newly religious Elvi fanatics would fight with their greatest asset: music. A weapon Agent Jones\'97and indeed all of Ulyssia\'97had no counter for. Hostilities had broken out already. Ulyssia was succumbing to a craze of Elvi-mania. Earwig melodies and enigmatic lyrics, reportedly written by someone they called the Great Teacher.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Could that be Anthony Chantara?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Credible reports had Chantara living in a large, recently built recording complex in the Ghetto.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tax in heaven!\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi could conquer the world. Pacifying the masses with music that had both sex appeal and emotional authenticity, then finishing them off with artery-hardening kilojoules and drugs. Don\'92t lose sight of the drugs.\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>We don\'92t stand a chance
\f2\i0 , Agent Jones realised, 
\f1\i unless we stop it now
\f2\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He grabbed another napkin. The bartender flinched.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones imagined it all in glittering, blinding rhinestones and bacon-flavoured horror: a holy jihad launched across the world by the Holy Momma, aided by the Great Teacher, and equipped by the greatest arms dealer in the world. Like those old sci-fi movies Agent Jones never saw. But instead of fierce Zen Sufi sand-warriors cutting the hearts out of their foes and taking their water, an army of Elvi would shake-rattle-and-roll their way to victory using the power of... entertainment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones almost gagged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Making it worse, Elvi men were persuasively handsome. And the women were, well, handsome too\'97many wore sideburns like the men. They were still very attractive, and it repulsed Agent Jones to his marrow.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender cleared his throat. \'93Sir, we\'92re, uh, running low on napkins.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones waved him away.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The more Agent Jones thought about it, the more he hoped the Elvi would do what the Elvi always did: resort to violence. Guns, tanks, pool cues, and broken toilet bowls were easier to fight than cultural memes and chart-topping tunes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It all connects.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What of Jihad Man?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Call it intuition. Agent Jones felt in his paranoid bones that Jihad Man was part of this somehow. Which wasn\'92t surprising. All the psychometric instruments PAPER\'92s inhuman resources department used effectively selected for candidates suffering from apophenia\'97the human tendency to see imaginary patterns. Which also explained why many of them were religious nuts, seeing P&L charts in the clouds that predicted the future, and so on.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones had performed so well on these tests, his scores had slipped straight off the bell curve and into some other statistical reality entirely. Which made Agent Jones a darling of the upper echelons of PAPER and the political elite who interacted with it. When something messy needed doing, just aim Agent Jones and sit back while he derived all kinds of interconnections in the service of justifying his mission, which he rarely failed to accomplish.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But Agent Jones was failing to accomplish this mission, and so he resorted to searching for ever more elaborate patterns, involving an ever-increasing cast of bad actors. A kind of Grand Unified Theory of Evil, you might say, though Agent Jones would never think of it in these terms. To him, Evil was a singular amorphous toxic cloud in which particulates were all connected, if only in an amorphous, cloudy way.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>You need to see the cloud and all the little clouds inside it, he told himself, scribbling faster.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Which is all to say, whether or not there was any factual relationship between Gerry Hardman and Eloise Shakti: given enough time, pencils and napkins, Agent Jones was hard-wired to see one.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Of course, the obvious link was Alexander Shakti, Eloise\'92s TRAMPS officer son\'97but everyone knew Shakti was an idiot, and Eloise kept her business at so many arms\'92 lengths from her son they could span the continent.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On impulse, Agent Jones grabbed another napkin and wrote:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jihad of the Elvi
\f2\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He was pretty sure that was a safe thing to write. Anyone reading it would just think him crazy, which most people thought anyway. There\'92d be no need to moisten and swallow the napkin. He contemplated the phrase, then wrote underneath:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Elvian Jihad
\f2\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kind of like a Butlerian Jihad, except with entertainers instead of butlers. Then his eyes went wide and he wrote:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Elvian Jihad Man
\f2\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones\'92 blood cooled. He felt a frisson tingle down his neck and flow all the way to his nethers, a sense of the jigsaw falling into place\'97which a therapist should have counselled him years ago could be his apophenia assuming control of his lizard brain. 
\f1\i Thar be dragons!
\f2\i0  the therapist\'97any therapist\'97should have warned, dragons being nothing more than a taxing big lizard with a tiny lizard brain.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry Hardman was the lizard in the room; Agent Jones was sure of it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He didn\'92t trust Hardman from the moment he\'92d turned the war cabinet into a personal audience with the CEO. Agent Jones still had no knowledge of what they\'92d discussed; not even he had security clearance for the bugs in the most secure office in the land. How had such a recently promoted Police Commissioner exerted such influence over the CEO of Ulyssia?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Suspicious.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>So was the previous Commissioner\'92s retirement. The man had only recently been appointed when the CEO gained power. Retirements were not, of themselves, suspicious. But the burning of effigies on the lawn of the Commissioner\'92s church\'97dressed as white supremacists, and which forensics found were stuffed with dog-digested human remains\'97starkly marked the transition of the previous Commissioner from \'91presumed on vacation\'92 to \'91presumed retired\'92.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then the CEO had fired the Director of Freedom within days of the war cabinet and hadn\'92t bothered replacing him. The Department of Freedom had direct oversight of PAPER, which meant Agent Jones\'92 own chain of command now vanished into a hole so dark even his security clearance couldn\'92t illuminate it. PAPER seemed neither Proactive nor Preemptive\'97and nobody seemed to care that the dog was off the leash.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And Gerry Hardman had all the scent of a double agent. Agent Jones could smell double agents, even under the Layer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender was hovering nearby, pretending to arrange bottles, stealing glances at the mountain of crumpled napkins in the bin.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And when Agent Jones had read-in the fresh Commissioner Hardman on relevant high-profile national security issues, he\'92d been surprised when no busy-body probing questions came. Not a single question out of order.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Probably because Jihad Man knew the answers to all of them already.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>None of this was coincidence, Agent Jones concluded, because there were no coincidences. The only coincidence he might credit?\'97when someone said something smart-mouthed and then he knocked their teeth out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Now, that was the best coincidence of all.
